dpoint coupling, bridge law, and sampler-visible state. For a deterministic Monge OT coupling with a straight bridge, the full-state gap is zero because the current state identifies the endpoint pairing. Noisy OT and independent couplings have positive gaps. With the same Monge endpoint coupling, switching from a straight bridge to a Brownian bridge yields positive gap, showing that the diagnostic varies along the bridge/target axis. With the same straight Monge bridge, projecting the visible state from 3D to 2D or 1D creates positive gap while endpoint cost and velocity scale are unchanged. Thus the gap is not reducible to endpoint transport cost or path length; it depends on the bridge law and the information visible to the Markov decoder.\n\nTo verify that the analytic quantity is the regression floor, we also train a small Markov decoder to predict the local target U_t from the visible state S_t and time. The oracle Gaussian conditional mean attains the exact average gap, and the learned MLP approaches this MSE across the tested configurations. This directly supports the revised definition G(Q,U,O), where U is the local target and O defines the sampler-visible state.\n\"\"\".strip()\n(OUT_DIR / 'rebuttal_exact3d_neural_paragraph.txt').write_text(rebuttal)\nprint(rebuttal)\nprint('\\nSaved all outputs to', OUT_DIR)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T02:37:22.328931Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T02:37:22.329279Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T02:37:22.336294Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T02:37:22.329251Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T02:37:22.335299Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"We added an exact 3D Gaussian--OT diagnostic and a neural regression sanity check to address the proxy and novelty concerns. In this controlled Euclidean setting, the Markovization gap is computed analytically from Gaussian conditional covariances, not by a kNN/PCA proxy. The experiment varies the endpoint coupling, bridge law, and sampler-visible state. For a deterministic Monge OT coupling with a straight bridge, the full-state gap is zero because the current state identifies the endpoint pairing. Noisy OT and independent couplings have positive gaps. With the same Monge endpoint coupling, switching from a straight bridge to a Brownian bridge yields positive gap, showing that the diagnostic varies along the bridge/target axis. With the same straight Monge bridge, projecting the visible state from 3D to 2D or 1D creates positive gap while endpoint cost and velocity scale are unchanged. Thus the gap is not reducible to endpoint transport cost or path length; it depends on the bridge law and the information visible to the Markov decoder.\n\nTo verify that the analytic quantity is the regression floor, we also train a small Markov decoder to predict the local target U_t from the visible state S_t and time. The oracle Gaussian conditional mean attains the exact average gap, and the learned MLP approaches this MSE across the tested configurations. This directly supports the revised definition G(Q,U,O), where U is the local target and O defines the sampler-visible state.\n\nSaved all outputs to /kaggle/working/bgm_3d_markovization_neural\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":11},{"id":"3096f973-2387-403d-8e7c-78eb7fd25129","cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}